<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management for CBIX.
    Every PackageReference version lives here; .csproj files carry the package
    identity only. Design §11 requires exact pinned versions (MAF and its Anthropic
    integration are young and prerelease) - so no floating or wildcard versions.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Runtime">
    <!-- The LLM-neutral currency of the whole solution (design 3): AIAgent/IChatClient and
         the AIContent hierarchy live here, and MAF builds on them. Cbix.Core depends on
         these abstractions ONLY - never on a provider package - so a provider swap stays a
         configuration change. Provider-specific payloads ride inside a profile via
         AIContent.RawRepresentation, never in a Core signature. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Abstractions" Version="10.9.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- Logging abstractions only - ILogger/ILogger<T>, no sink, no provider. Cbix.Core needs it
         because a security refusal that is only an exception is invisible until something catches
         it: the ingest containment gate has to emit a structured event at the moment it refuses.
         Neutral by construction (it names no model provider), which is why adding it to
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist is a deliberate, commented act rather than a
         loophole. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!-- The local PDF text layer (design 5.1), and the corpus the validator's grounding gate
         does verbatim string containment against (design 5.6). Latest STABLE line: 0.1.15,
         released 2026-06-25; the 0.1.16-alpha-* stream on nuget.org is the project's rolling
         CI feed and is not a release.

         Why this belongs in Cbix.Core and does not weaken LLM-agnosticism: PdfPig is a local,
         offline, Apache-2.0 PDF parsing library. It is a neutral document library, not a
         provider SDK - it names no model provider, makes no network call, and is the same
         dependency under every provider profile (the Claude native-PDF profile, the generic
         vision profile and the text-only fallback all need this text layer). Adding it to
         CoreAssemblyNeutralityTests' allowlist is therefore the deliberate act that list's
         procedure describes, not a loophole.

         Transitive closure: NONE on our target framework. PdfPig 0.1.15's nuspec declares
         dependencies only for its .NET Framework 4.6.2/4.7.1 and netstandard2.0 groups
         (Microsoft.Bcl.HashCode, System.Memory, System.ValueTuple - all polyfills); the
         net6.0 and net8.0 groups are empty, and net10.0 resolves the net8.0 group. The
         package does ship seven assemblies in one lib folder (UglyToad.PdfPig plus .Core,
         .DocumentLayoutAnalysis, .Fonts, .Package, .Tokenization, .Tokens), so the
         neutrality allowlist names the ones Core actually emits a reference to, not the
         package id.

         No bracket pin, and no claim that the version is safe because the package is
         "stable": it is 0.1.x, and a 0.x scheme explicitly disclaims a stability promise.
         What actually holds this version still is mechanical - the committed
         packages.lock.json plus CI's locked restore, which fail the build if the resolved
         version moves for any reason.

         Upgrade procedure, and it is not a formality: this package IS the grounding corpus.
         A PdfPig release that changes reading order, word-gap inference or whitespace by so
         much as one character changes the text every SourceSnippet is checked against, and
         that drift is invisible in a build, a unit test and a diff alike - it surfaces only
         as grounding-gate failures on documents that used to pass. So a version bump
         requires a green golden-set run before it ships (CLAUDE.md quality gate), and until
         the eval harness exists there is no way to see the damage at all. Bump deliberately
         or not at all. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.15" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Provider adapter (Anthropic) - story S01-08">
    <!--
      Both MAF packages are referenced DIRECTLY, not left to arrive through the Anthropic
      integration, because between them they declare the type the adapter's public signature
      returns - the neutral currency the rest of the solution speaks. A type in our own API
      surface should not arrive by accident.
        - Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions declares AIAgent itself (verified: the S01-08
          scenario asserts on the declaring assembly, and caught the assumption that it lived
          in Microsoft.Agents.AI).
        - Microsoft.Agents.AI declares ChatClientAgent, the concrete type AsAIAgent hands back
          and which the adapter widens to AIAgent.
      Note the version scheme: both core packages are STABLE at 1.17.0 while the Anthropic
      integration below is still prerelease. That maturity gap is exactly the design 11
      caution, and it is why the integration is quarantined in its own assembly.

      Bracket notation is deliberate here and nowhere else in this file. A bare version in
      NuGet is a MINIMUM, not an exact pin; the entries above accept that because they are
      mature packages on a stable line. Design 11 requires exact pinned versions for MAF
      and its provider integration specifically, and `[x]` is the only notation that
      actually delivers one for a local (unlocked) restore. CI's locked restore would catch
      drift after the fact; the bracket prevents it in the first place.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI.Abstractions" Version="[1.17.0]" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI" Version="[1.17.0]" />
    <!--
      MAF's first-party Anthropic integration (design 7). PRERELEASE - there is no stable
      line to move to, which is the whole reason design 11 says to pin exactly and expect
      breaking changes on upgrade. This version resolves Microsoft.Agents.AI 1.17.0 and
      the official Anthropic .NET SDK 12.35.1 (pinned directly below).

      Upgrade procedure: bump this pin, check whether the integration's dependency group
      moved the `Anthropic` version and bump that pin to match, then require a green
      golden-set run before shipping (CLAUDE.md quality gate).
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Agents.AI.Anthropic" Version="[1.17.0-preview.260804.1]" />
    <!--
      The official Anthropic .NET SDK. Pinned exactly and referenced DIRECTLY by the adapter,
      which is an accurate statement of what the code does: `AsAIAgent` is an extension on
      IAnthropicClient, so the adapter must construct an AnthropicClient and its ClientOptions
      itself. Those SDK types are named in AnthropicAgentFactory today.

      Two things this pin does NOT do, stated plainly because an earlier version of this
      comment got both wrong:
        - It does not weaken containment. Containment was never a property of the package
          graph: a transitively-supplied assembly is fully compilable-against, so any project
          could have named these types whether or not it declared a PackageReference. What
          actually enforces containment is the reference-table assertion in
          ProviderContainmentTests / the S01-08 BDD scenario, which fails if any assembly
          outside the adapter emits a reference to `Anthropic` or
          `Microsoft.Agents.AI.Anthropic`.
        - Conversely, leaving it transitive did not pin the version. The MAF integration
          declares `Anthropic >= 12.35.1`, so a lock regeneration would float it upward
          silently. The exact pin plus CI's locked restore is what holds the version still -
          which matters because the adapter depends on measured SDK behaviour (see the
          ambient-credential remarks on AnthropicProviderOptions).
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Anthropic" Version="[12.35.1]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test">
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
    <!-- Runtime-conditional skips. xUnit v2 cannot skip from inside a test, so a probe whose
         precondition the host denies (creating a symbolic link needs a Windows privilege) would
         otherwise have to `return` silently and report itself as passed - a security probe that
         lies. Pinned to the version Reqnroll.xUnit already resolves transitively, so the graph
         gains no new resolution. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Xunit.SkippableFact" Version="1.4.13" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="BDD">
    <!-- Reqnroll.xUnit is the single entry point: it pulls in Reqnroll and
         Reqnroll.Tools.MsBuild.Generation (the .feature -> .feature.cs generator).
         3.3.4 depends on xunit.core 2.8.1, satisfied by our xunit 2.9.3. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Reqnroll.xUnit" Version="3.3.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
